<title>UK: CZARNIKOW SEES WORLD SUGAR STOCKS FALLING IN 97/98.</title>
<headline>CZARNIKOW SEES WORLD SUGAR STOCKS FALLING IN 97/98.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-08-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>World sugar stocks should fall by 1.87 million tonnes raw value over the coming 1997/98 crop cycle, Czarnikow Sugar said on Thursday.</p>
<p>This compared with a revised increase in stocks of 370,000 tonnes estimated for 1996/97, it said in a report. Czarnikow gave no figures for sugar stocks.</p>
<p>Global sugar consumption was estimated at 124.37 million tonnes, which had to be added to the 750,000 tonnes of sugar allowed for unrecorded disappearance.</p>
<p>Consumption reached 121.96 million tonnes in 1997, Czarnikow said.</p>
<p>Sugar production was pegged at 123.25 million tonnes for 1997/98, slightly higher than the 123.08 million tonnes turned out the previous season.</p>
<p>-- Jeremy Smith, London Newsroom +44 171 542 8064</p>
</text>
